    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Grades are now dated, and all 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it works now**\n- **Storage:** a new migration, `V19__person_grade.sql`, adds a `person_grade` table that keeps one grade per person per from-date. A blank from-date means \"from the beginning of time\". Each person's current grade is moved into it as their first grade, and the old `person.grade` column is dropped.\n- **Person form:** it has a new from-date field (`person-form-grade-from`), which shows the sheet's today unless you type a date. Saving a grade records it from that date and keeps the earlier ones; a second grade saved for the same date replaces the first. A new person's first grade applies from the beginning of time.\n- **Which grade is used:**\n  - A claim's due uses the grade in force on the claim's date. This applies on the server (rollup, statements, settling a claim) and on screen (claim rows, people totals, periods).\n  - The list shows the grade in force on the sheet's today.\n  - `PERSON_EDITED` and `PERSON_ADDED` record that same today grade, taken after the save.\n\n**One behaviour change the test required.** A person's due-total used to show n/a whenever their current grade had no rate today, unless a claim had a settled or statement-bound amount. Now it shows n/a only if, in addition, none of their counting claims has an amount at all. Older claims can now carry a different grade that does have a rate, so the total adds up whatever amounts exist. The first test expects exactly this: 3.00, not n/a, after Ada moves to grade C, which has no rate. I changed the rollup, statement and screen code to match.\n\n**Not done:** the request mentions import lines, but the app has no import feature and the test doesn't cover one, so there was nothing to change. The closest thing, quick-add creating a new person, gives that person their first grade from the beginning of time.",
